response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use procedure with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify steps and explain why it matters.","I can practise imperative independently on a new prompt.","I can notice order needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Procedures teach how to do something using a goal, materials and ordered steps, often with imperative verbs. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Procedures teach how to do something using a goal, materials and ordered steps, often with imperative verbs.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about procedure?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"Goal: Make lemon drink. Materials: lemon, water, cup. Steps: 1. Wash lemon. 2. Squeeze juice. 3. Add water. 4. Stir.\" Clear order and commands.\n\nTRY FIRST\nOrder these steps: stir; add water; squeeze lemon; wash lemon.\n\nFEEDBACK\nWash lemon → squeeze → add water → stir.","explain":"Procedures teach how to do something using a goal, materials and ordered steps, often with imperative verbs.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Missing materials or scrambled steps make a procedure hard to follow. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Procedure goal with ordered imperative steps.","caption":"Order makes procedures usable.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Goal</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">make lemon drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Steps</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">wash → squeeze → water → stir</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"Goal: Make lemon drink. Materials: lemon, water, cup. Steps: 1. Wash lemon. 2. Squeeze juice. 3. Add water. 4. Stir.\" C Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"Goal: Make lemon drink. Materials: lemon, water, cup. Steps: 1. Wash lemon. 2. Squeeze juice. 3. Add water. 4. Stir.\" C Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about procedure?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"Goal: Make lemon drink. Materials: lemon, water, cup. Steps: 1. Wash lemon. 2. Squeeze juice. 3. Add water. 4. Stir.\" Clear order and commands. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Clear commands contrasted with a broken procedure.","caption":"Materials and sequence both matter."