 the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Missing materials or scrambled steps make a procedure hard to follow. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about procedure?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nProcedures teach how to do something using a goal, materials and ordered steps, often with imperative verbs. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 26, Lesson 1: Reading procedures","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Procedures and explanations","week":26,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n\"Goal: Make lemon drink. Materials: lemon, water, cup. Steps: 1. Wash lemon. 2. Squeeze juice. 3. Add water. 4. Stir.\" Clear order and commands.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Procedure goal with ordered imperative steps.","caption":"Order makes procedures usable.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Goal</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">make lemon drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Steps</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">wash → squeeze → water → stir</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Clear commands contrasted with a broken procedure.","caption":"Materials and sequence both matter.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"